<body>

    <div class="header">
        <h1>SAKARYA UNIVERSITY</h1>
        <h2>Faculty of Computer and Information Sciences - Department of Software Engineering</h2>
        <h3>SWE301 System Programming Course | 2026-2027 Fall Semester</h3>
        <h2 style="margin-top: 10px; color: #003366;">Term Project 1: POSIX Signal-Based Process Monitoring and Management System (Watchdog)</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-title">1. Project Architecture and System Design</div>
    <p>
        This project implements a supervisor program (referred to as the <strong>watchdog</strong>) that creates, monitors, and manages a set of child processes using standard POSIX system calls. The application tracks the life cycle of its child processes, detects crashes, restarts processes that terminate unexpectedly within a defined limit (at most 3 times), responds safely to asynchronous POSIX signals (<code>SIGINT</code>, <code>SIGTERM</code>, <code>SIGCHLD</code>, <code>SIGUSR1</code>, <code>SIGALRM</code>), and logs all lifecycle events with precise timestamps.
    </p>

    <div class="sub-title">1.1. Modular System Structure</div>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signal Manager & Main Loop (<code>src/main.c</code>, <code>src/signal_manager.c</code>):</strong> Installs signal handlers via <code>sigaction()</code>, maps asynchronous events to async-signal-safe flags, coordinates the main event polling loop, and orchestrates graceful shutdown.</li>
        <li><strong>Process Manager (<code>src/process_manager.c</code>, <code>workers/worker.c</code>):</strong> Creates worker processes via <code>fork()</code> and <code>execv()</code>, maintains the process status table, reaps terminated processes via non-blocking <code>waitpid()</code>, and sets up interval timers for health monitoring.</li>
        <li><strong>Restart Manager (<code>src/restart_manager.c</code>):</strong> Evaluates termination causes, enforces the strict maximum 3 restart limit per process, avoids infinite restart loops, and implements the 5-second shutdown timeout with <code>SIGKILL</code> fallback.</li>
        <li><strong>Logger Module (<code>src/logger.c</code>):</strong> Writes timestamped lifecycle records to <code>watchdog.log</code> and formats comprehensive child process status reports upon receiving <code>SIGUSR1</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="sub-title">1.2. Required POSIX Mechanisms and System Calls</div>
    <ul>
        <li><strong><code>sigaction()</code> & Async-Signal Safety:</strong> Handlers are strictly async-signal-safe. Complex I/O or memory allocation is avoided inside handlers; they only record state transitions in <code>volatile sig_atomic_t</code> flags, delegating processing to the main loop.</li>
        <li><strong><code>fork()</code>, <code>execv()</code>, and <code>_exit()</code>:</strong> Workers are spawned using <code>fork()</code> and replaced via <code>execv()</code>. On execution failure, <code>_exit(EXIT_FAILURE)</code> is called to prevent duplicate flushing of inherited standard I/O buffers.</li>
        <li><strong><code>waitpid()</code> (WNOHANG & EINTR):</strong> Reaps finished children to prevent zombie processes. It handles <code>EINTR</code> signals and differentiates normal exit (<code>WIFEXITED</code>, exit code 0) from application errors (exit code != 0) and uncaught signals (<code>WIFSIGNALED</code>).</li>
        <li><strong><code>setitimer()</code> & <code>SIGALRM</code>:</strong> Sets a real-time interval timer (<code>ITIMER_REAL</code>) for periodic process table health inspections and timeout enforcement.</li>
        <li><strong><code>kill()</code>:</strong> Dispatches termination signals (<code>SIGTERM</code>, <code>SIGKILL</code>) with error validation.</li>
    </ul>

